\section{Temperature Ablation}\label{app:t_ablation}

Table~\ref{tab:t_ablation} reports the effect of sampling temperature on QE signals, oracle headroom, and output quality across four temperatures on SciERC NER.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Temperature ablation on SciERC NER (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, seed${=}42$). Oracle gap and QE $\rho$ across four temperatures. $\kappa$: Fleiss' $\kappa$ (inter-sample agreement). $T{=}0.5$ and $T{=}1.0$ use joint (NER+RE) subtask; $T{=}0.8$ and $T{=}1.2$ use NER-only.}
\label{tab:t_ablation}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{ccccccc}
\toprule
$T$ & Oracle gap (pp) & SJ $\rho$ & LP $\rho$ & $\kappa$ & Sample F1 \\
\midrule
0.5 & $+$9.1 & 0.297 & 0.218 & 0.577 & 0.636 \\
0.8 & $+$12.5 & 0.359 & 0.253 & 0.451 & 0.622 \\
1.0 & $+$13.0 & 0.360 & 0.205 & ${\approx}$0.39 & ${\approx}$0.612 \\
1.2 & $+$14.6 & 0.377 & 0.196 & 0.356 & 0.594 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

Oracle headroom increases monotonically with temperature ($+9.1$~pp at $T{=}0.5$ to $+14.6$~pp at $T{=}1.2$), confirming that higher temperature consistently expands the oracle ceiling under constrained structured decoding.
Structural QE signals improve with temperature: SJ $\rho$ rises from $0.297$ ($T{=}0.5$) to $0.377$ ($T{=}1.2$), consistent with more diverse outputs providing richer pairwise evidence.
LP $\rho$ peaks at $T{=}0.8$ ($0.253$) then degrades to $0.196$ at $T{=}1.2$.
This divergence confirms that LP compression is model-intrinsic: higher temperature increases between-instance LP variance (benefiting structural signals) but does not resolve within-instance compression, degrading LP's discriminative power.
Inter-sample agreement ($\kappa$) decreases monotonically ($0.577$ to $0.356$); higher temperature generates more diverse outputs. Mean sample F1 also decreases ($0.636$ to $0.594$), consistent with the quality--diversity trade-off.
The $T{=}0.7$ and $T{=}1.0$ comparison in Table~\ref{tab:robustness} is consistent with the trends reported here.

\paragraph{Temperature controls degeneracy and LP spread.}
Table~\ref{tab:t_degeneracy} reports instance degeneracy (constant F1, gold-filtered) and median within-instance LP range across four temperatures on SciERC.
Both quantities are strictly monotonic in $T$: degeneracy falls from 41.4\% ($T{=}0.5$) to 14.9\% ($T{=}1.2$), while LP range increases from 0.015 to 0.058 nats.
Temperature thus provides a direct lever for practitioners to shift the degeneracy regime.
However, even at $T{=}1.2$ where degeneracy (14.9\%) approaches Few-NERD levels (${\sim}$13\%), within-instance $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1})$ remains at 0.225, far below Few-NERD's ${\sim}$0.5.
This confirms that temperature controls degeneracy but cannot improve LP-quality alignment, which is largely determined by task structure and remains relatively stable across generation configurations (\S\ref{app:epoch_confound}). LP-quality alignment, not degeneracy, is the primary predictor of LP selection direction (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

